\subsection{Errors, flags and signal numbers}

The kernel reports an error as \texttt{-1} and keeps the number in the process (as the book does); Linux wants the negative number as
the result. The error numbers the book knows are the same as Linux's, except \texttt{EAGAIN} (35 in the book, 11 on Linux). When
the kernel gave no number, the call that failed names a default.

<<linux_abi.c: errors, flags and signal numbers>>=
#define LE_EPERM    1
#define LE_ENOENT   2
#define LE_ESRCH    3
#define LE_EINTR    4
#define LE_EIO      5
#define LE_ECHILD  10
#define LE_EAGAIN  11
#define LE_ENOMEM  12
#define LE_EACCES  13
#define LE_EFAULT  14
#define LE_EBADF    9
#define LE_EINVAL  22
#define LE_ENOTTY  25
#define LE_ERANGE  34
#define LE_ENOSYS  38

static long linux_error (int dflt) {
  int e = get_errno ();
  if (e == EAGAIN) e = LE_EAGAIN;
  return -(e ? e : dflt);
}

#define AT_FDCWD  (-100)
#define LO_CREAT   0100                  // Linux/AArch64 values of the open flags
#define LO_APPEND  02000

@
The book numbers its signals like BSD (\texttt{SIGUSR1} is 30, \texttt{SIGSTOP} 17), musl like Linux. \texttt{to\_ulix[n]} is the
book's number for Linux signal \texttt{n}, 0 if the book has no such signal; \texttt{to\_linux} is the table the other way round.

<<linux_abi.c: errors, flags and signal numbers>>=
static const char to_ulix[32] = {
  0,  1,  2,  3,  4,  5,  6, 10,  8,  9, 30, 11, 31, 13, 14, 15,      //  0..15:  Linux 7 = SIGBUS, 10 = SIGUSR1, 12 = SIGUSR2
  0, 20, 19, 17, 18, 21, 22, 16, 24, 25, 26, 27,  0, 23,  0, 12 };    // 16..31:  17 = SIGCHLD, 18 = SIGCONT, 19 = SIGSTOP, 20 = SIGTSTP
static signed char to_linux[32];

static void signal_tables (void) {
  if (to_linux[1]) return;
  for (int l = 1; l < 32; l++) if (to_ulix[l]) to_linux[(int) to_ulix[l]] = l;
}

// the number a program of musl knows for the book's signal number (called when a signal is delivered)
int linux_signal_number (int signo) {
  if (!linux_abi_as[current_as]) return signo;
  signal_tables ();
  return to_linux[signo] ? to_linux[signo] : signo;
}

u64 linux_signal_restorer (void) { return linux_sig_restorer[current_as]; }

